title:: reLation
summary:: Musical objects and the relations that move them: pitch systems, chords, transformations, walks, and maps between contexts
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLEDO, Classes/RLJust, Classes/RLChordSpace, Classes/RLChordContext, Classes/RLTransform, Classes/RLWalk, Classes/RLMorphism, Classes/RLVoiceLeading, Classes/RLRelation, Classes/RLRelationContext, Classes/RLCategory, Classes/RLGraph, Classes/RLQuantale, Classes/RLNetwork, Classes/RLAutomorphism, Classes/RLExtension, Classes/RLWreath, Classes/RLTimeSpans, Classes/RLMetre, Classes/RLPulse, Classes/RLTimePoints, Classes/RLTimeMap

section:: What it is

reLation (classes code::RL...::) is transformational music theory as objects: harmony is described by what moves one chord to another, and a progression is a walk, a start and a list of moves. Four notions carry everything:

list::
## a strong::context:: : musical objects and the transformations that move them (link::Classes/RLChordContext::: the triads of a tuning, moved by T, I, P, L, R);
## a strong::walk:: : a start and a word, the list of its steps (link::Classes/RLWalk::);
## a strong::morphism:: : a map from one context to another that the transformations go through (link::Classes/RLMorphism::: a temperament, from the just lattice to an equal division);
## a strong::lift:: : a walk of the target read back in the source, where a walk that closed may end a comma away.
::

Nothing is tied to 12 notes: a context is built on a link::Classes/RLPitchSystem::, an equal division (link::Classes/RLEDO::) or just intonation (link::Classes/RLJust::), and the same word is the same walk in each. The theory, its sources and the numbers the tests check are in HomewareSC, code::Math/Category_Theory/THEORY.md::.

section:: How to read each object

Coordinates are plain data, read by the system they belong to; everything with rules of its own is a small object that checks them and compares by value.

table::
## what || how it is written || its rules
## a pitch class || an Integer in RLEDO (code::7::), an Array of exponents in RLJust (code::[1, 0]::, one per odd prime), the same in RLTempered, reduced by its commas || belongs to one system; add and compare through it (code::pcAdd::, code::cents::), never with code::+::
## a pitch || an Integer of steps in RLEDO, code::[e2, e3, e5]:: in RLJust and RLTempered || code::system.height(pitch):: is its place in octaves above the origin
## a voicing || an Array of pitches, one per voice || voice i stays voice i; never sorted
## a pitch system || code::RLEDO(19)::, code::RLJust([3, 5])::, code::RLTempered.meantone:: || a value
## a chord || link::Classes/RLChord::: a root (a pitch class) and a type || the type is one of its space's; no octave, no voicing; prints as its name
## a chord space || link::Classes/RLChordSpace::: a system and its chord types || a type is a name and a pattern, root first
## a context || link::Classes/RLChordContext:: || needs a triad and its inversion
## a transformation || link::Classes/RLTransform::: code::ctx.t(7)::, code::ctx.l::, code::ctx.l <> ctx.p:: || code::<>:: composes right to left; equal when they move every chord alike
## a word || an Array of Symbols or RLTransforms, code::[\R, \L, \P]:: || in the order of time: R first
## a walk || link::Classes/RLWalk::: a context, a start, a word || the start is a chord of the context
## a morphism || link::Classes/RLMorphism::: two contexts and a map of chords || code::isValid:: checks that it commutes with the named transformations
## a relation || link::Classes/RLRelation::: two lists of objects and a value for each pair || composes right to left when one starts on the list where the other ends; code::images(x):: is a list, code::value(x):: only where there is one image
## a quantale || code::RLQuantale.boolean::, code::.lawvere::, code::.multiplicative::, code::.interval:: || what the values of a relation are and how they combine
## a relation context || link::Classes/RLRelationContext::: objects and named relations || code::act:: answers a list of any length
## a category || link::Classes/RLCategory::: what named arrows generate || each arrow has a shortest word, in time order; its label reads right to left
## a graph || link::Classes/RLGraph::: the graph of a relation, with a cost on each edge || a path is a plain list of objects
## a network || link::Classes/RLNetwork::: nodes with one object each, arrows with labels || valid when each label takes its tail to its head and the labels compose as the arrows do
## an automorphism || link::Classes/RLAutomorphism::: a one-to-one map of a relation context's objects || valid when it turns every relation of the context into one of them
## a group element || an Array code::[z, h]:: read by an link::Classes/RLExtension:: (root difference and type difference from a base chord) || means a chord only once the base chord is named
## a uniform transformation || an Array code::[shifts, types]:: read by an link::Classes/RLWreath:: || one shift and one new type for each chord type
## a time-span || an Array code::[onset, duration]:: read by an link::Classes/RLTimeSpans:: || the duration above zero; also an element of the group that moves the spans
## a time point || a number read by an link::Classes/RLTimePoints:: (bars, beats, seconds) || compared within a billionth; fractions written as divisions, code::17/6::
## a pulse || link::Classes/RLPulse::: a period, code::RLPulse(1/6)::; as a label also the period alone or code::'M1/6':: || the period is a fraction; code::<>:: is the gcd, code::<=:: the inclusion
## a pulse layer || an Array code::[period, phase]::, or a period alone, read by an link::Classes/RLTimePoints:: || the points phase + k period
## a map of time || link::Classes/RLTimeMap::: code::RLTimeMap(a, b)::, t to a t + b || a is not 0
## a network of networks || an link::Classes/RLNetwork:: on the context link::Classes/RLRelationContext#*networks:: makes || its nodes hold networks of one shape, its labels are the names of the moves
::

Sizes are always in cents (intervals, drifts, voice motions), so that numbers from different systems compare.

section:: Chords and what moves them

code::
~ctx = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(12)));
~c = ~ctx.space.major(0);            // C major
~ctx.neighbours(~c);                 // [[P, C minor], [L, E minor], [R, A minor]]
~ctx.t(7).(~c);                      // G major
(~ctx.l <> ~ctx.p).(~c);             // Ab major: P first, then L
~ctx.rightInterval(~c, ~ctx.space.minor(9));    // R: what takes C major to A minor
~ctx.leftInterval(~c, ~ctx.space.minor(9));     // I4: the inversion that does
::

T and I act on the pitch classes, the same way for every chord; P, L, R are read from the chord they move (they keep two of its tones). The two families are the left and the right action of one group and commute: code::(~ctx.t(5) <> ~ctx.r) == (~ctx.r <> ~ctx.t(5))::.

section:: A walk in several tunings

code::
~pump = RLWalk(~ctx, ~c, [\R, \L, \R, \P, \R, \L, \R, \L]);
~pump.chords;                        // C major, A minor, F major, D minor, D major, B minor, G major, E minor, C major
~pump.isClosed;                      // true

~e19 = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(19)));
~pump.inContext(~e19).isClosed;      // true: 19 tempers the syntonic comma out too
~e22 = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(22)));
~pump.inContext(~e22).isClosed;      // false: 22 does not
~pump.inContext(~e22).driftCents;    // -54.5: one step of 22 down

~just = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLJust([3, 5])));
~lifted = ~pump.inContext(~just);
~lifted.drift;                       // [-4, 1]
~just.system.commaName(~lifted.drift);          // syntonic comma 81/80
::

The cycles of the usual transformations show what a tuning tempers out. In 12 steps P, L closes after 3 turns (the diesis 128/125 is tempered out), P, R after 4 (the major diesis 648/625), R, L after 12 (the Pythagorean comma); in 19 and 31 each needs as many turns as the division has steps.

code::
[12, 19, 22, 31].collect { |n|
	var ctx = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(n)));
	[[\P, \L], [\P, \R], [\R, \L]].collect { |w| RLWalk.cycle(ctx, ctx.space.major(0), w).size.div(2) }
};
// [[3, 4, 12], [19, 19, 19], [22, 11, 22], [31, 31, 31]]
::

section:: Temperaments and lifts

A temperament is a morphism from the lattice to a division: roots go through the division's val, P, L and R go through unchanged. A closed walk of the division lifts to a walk of the lattice that ends on a comma the division tempers out (link::Classes/RLJust#-kernel::).

code::
~temper = RLMorphism.temper(~just, ~ctx);
~temper.isValid;                                 // true
~hexatonic = RLWalk.cycle(~ctx, ~c, [\P, \L]);
~temper.lift(~hexatonic).drift;                  // [0, -3]: the diesis
~just.system.kernel(RLEDO(12), 4).collect { |x| ~just.system.commaName(x) };
::

link::Classes/RLChordContext#-closedWords:: lists every way round of a context up to a length: in 12 steps and at most eight, the hexatonic cycle, L P R twice, one more word, the pump and the octatonic cycle; in 19 and 31 only L P R twice and the pump. The same words on the lattice end on the commas each division hides, except L P R twice, which closes everywhere.

Between the lattice and a division stand the temperaments that give up some commas and not all (link::Classes/RLTempered::). Meantone gives up the syntonic comma: the plane of fifths and thirds becomes a chain of fifths, of which 12, 19 and 31 steps are tunings. What they share is seen there once: the pump closes whatever the fifth, and the three cycles all end on one pitch class, the diesis, which is one step in 19 and in 31 and nothing in 12.

code::
~mt = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.triads(RLTempered.meantone));         // quarter-comma
RLMorphism.temper(~just, ~mt).isValid;                                  // true: the lattice to meantone
RLMorphism.temper(~mt, ~ctx).isValid;                                   // true: meantone to 12 steps
~drift = RLWalk(~mt, ~mt.space.major, [\P, \L, \P, \L, \P, \L]).drift;  // [0, -3]
~mt.system.cents(~drift);  ~mt.system.commaName(~drift);                // 41.06 cents, the diesis 128/125
RLTempered.meantone.divisions(34);                                      // [5, 7, 12, 19, 24, 26, 31]
::

section:: Voices

A walk's chords have no octave; its voices do. link::Classes/RLWalk#-voices:: leads each voice by the least motion (link::Classes/RLVoiceLeading::). A walk can close on its chords and not on its voices: the pump comes back one inversion higher, the hexatonic cycle exactly in place.

code::
~pump.voiceDrift([60, 64, 67]);      // [4, 3, 5]
~hexatonic.voiceDrift([60, 64, 67]); // [0, 0, 0]
~pump.voiceCents;                    // [200, 100, 200, 100, 200, 100, 200, 100]: R moves a tone, L and P a semitone
~lifted.voiceCents;                  // 182.4 (10/9) for R, 111.7 (16/15) for L, 70.7 (25/24) for P
::

With more voices than a chord has tones, some tones are doubled, again by the least motion (link::Classes/RLVoiceLeading#*minimalDoubled::); link::Classes/RLVoiceLeading#*withDoublings:: leads the tones of one chord to those of another of any size.

code::
~pump.voices([48, 60, 64, 67]).last;     // four voices on triads
RLVoiceLeading.withDoublings(RLEDO(12), [0, 4, 7], [0, 4, 7, 10]);     // a triad to a seventh chord
::

section:: Relations: when a step offers several chords

P, L and R send each triad to one triad. Many musical relations do not: an augmented triad is one semitone from six triads. An link::Classes/RLRelation:: holds such a relation, link::Classes/RLRelationContext:: a family of them by name. Cube Dance is the 24 triads and the 4 augmented triads with the three relations U, P, L that move one voice by one semitone.

code::
~cd = RLRelationContext.cubeDance;
~sp = ~cd.space;
~cd.act(\U, ~sp.chord(0, \aug));         // six triads
~cd.act(\U, ~sp.major(0));               // [C aug]
~near = RLRelation.voiceMoves(~cd.objects, [1]);      // one voice by one step, however it is named
~near == ~cd.union.without(~cd.identityTransform);    // true: U, P and L together
(~near <> ~near).images(~sp.major(0)).size;           // 7 chords are two such steps away
::

The relations of a context generate a monoid (link::Classes/RLCategory::): all the different things a word can do. U, P, L generate 40 relations; six of them can be undone, the products of P and L; six relate D major to G minor.

code::
~m = ~cd.category;
~m.size;                                              // 40
~m.arrow([\U, \U, \U]) == ~m.generator(\U);           // true
~m.relating(~sp.major(2), ~sp.minor(7)).collect(_.label);    // UU, UPU ...
::

A relation can carry values (link::Classes/RLQuantale::): costs that add along a walk, strengths that a walk keeps the weakest of.

section:: Graphs and the two distances

The graph of a relation (link::Classes/RLGraph::) answers what a walker asks: how far, by which way, and whether one can pass through every chord once.

code::
~g = ~cd.graph;
~g.distance(~sp.major(0), ~sp.major(6));              // 6
~g.shortestPaths(~sp.major(0), ~sp.major(1));         // two walks of three steps
~g.countHamiltonianCycles;                            // 1296
~cd.wordOf(~g.hamiltonianCycle);                      // the steps of the first one, by name
~voices = RLVoiceLeading.through(~g.hamiltonianCycle, [60, 64, 67]);   // and its voices, one semitone at each step
::

Two distances between chords meet here: the number of steps in a graph, and the least voice leading (link::Classes/RLVoiceLeading#*stepsBetween::). On Cube Dance they are equal for every pair of chords, and so they are on its analogues in 15, 18 and 24 steps (code::RLRelationContext.cubeDance(RLEDO(15))::). On the 24 triads joined by P, L, R they are not: C major to C# major is 4 edges, 5 semitones along them, 3 semitones directly.

code::
~cw = RLRelationContext.triads(RLEDO(12));
~cw.graph.countHamiltonianCycles;                     // 62
~cw.graph.distance(~cw.space.major(0), ~cw.space.major(1));                          // 4
RLGraph.voiceLeading(~cw.union).distance(~cw.space.major(0), ~cw.space.major(1));    // 5
RLVoiceLeading.stepsBetween(~cw.space.major(0), ~cw.space.major(1));                 // 3
::

section:: Networks and isographies

A network (link::Classes/RLNetwork::) holds objects at its nodes and labels on its arrows, and is valid when it is a true picture of the context. Networks compare by their labels, whatever their notes: Webern's three trichords in op. 11 no. 2 are not transpositions or inversions of one another, and all three are the network I8, I9, T1.

code::
~pcs = RLRelationContext.pitchClasses;           // T and I on pitch classes
~net = { |x, y, z| RLNetwork(~pcs, [[\x, x], [\y, y], [\z, z]], [[\x, \y, \I8], [\y, \z, \I9], [\x, \z, \T1]]) };
~w1 = ~net.(9, 11, 10);  ~w2 = ~net.(1, 7, 2);  ~w3 = ~net.(5, 3, 6);     // (A, B, Bb), (C#, G, D), (F, Eb, F#)
[~w1, ~w2, ~w3].collect(_.isValid);              // all true
~w1.fillings(\x).size;                           // 12 networks have this structure
::

An strong::isography:: moves a network to another of the same shape. A complete one moves the whole context at once (link::Classes/RLAutomorphism::): contents and labels together, any network. A local one works node by node.

code::
~w1.transformedBy(RLAutomorphism.affine(~pcs, 1, 4));          // every note up 4: labels I4, I5, T1
~w1.localIsographies(~w2).collect { |l| l.collect { |p| ~pcs.nameOf(p[1]) } };   // T4, T8, T4 or I10, I6, I0
~pcs.category.automorphisms.size;                              // 48, of which the maps x -> kx + c realise 24
::

Applied again and again to a small network of chords, one automorphism makes a progression. Muse's "Take a Bow" is the cell D major, D augmented, G minor (U, then U) moved a fourth up each time; other automorphisms of Cube Dance change the labels too (U into PUP) and give other sequences from the same cell.

code::
~cd = RLRelationContext.cubeDance;
~sp = ~cd.space;
~cell = RLNetwork.chain(~cd, [~sp.major(2), ~sp.chord(2, \aug), ~sp.minor(7)], [\U, \U]);
~seq = ~cell.iterate(RLAutomorphism.transposition(~cd, 5), 11);
~chords = ~seq.collect(_.objects).flat;          // 36 chords
~voices = RLVoiceLeading.through(~chords);       // each step moves one voice up a semitone
~cd.category.automorphisms.size;                 // 12 ways of changing the labels
::

A network is a value, so a node of another network can hold it. link::Classes/RLRelationContext#*networks:: makes the context of that other network: the networks one network becomes under some moves, and the moves as relations between them. A network on it is a network of networks, checked as any network is, and the same step again gives the level above. A move is an automorphism of the first context (notes and labels together), or an isography alone (Lewin's <T1>, <I5>, named by link::Classes/RLRelationContext#-isography::: the labels move and the notes are free).

code::
// a cell, C#, Eb, G under I4, I10, T6, and a plan with the cell's own labels: the notes are moved by I2, I5
// and T3, which move the labels as <I4>, <I10> and <T6> do
~cell = RLNetwork(~pcs, [[\x, 1], [\y, 3], [\z, 7]], [[\x, \y, \I4], [\y, \z, \I10], [\x, \z, \T6]]);
~moves = [[\i2, RLAutomorphism.affine(~pcs, 11, 2)], [\i5, RLAutomorphism.affine(~pcs, 11, 5)], [\t3, RLAutomorphism.affine(~pcs, 1, 3)]];
~hyper = RLRelationContext.networks(~cell, ~moves);                   // 8 networks, moved by a group of 8
~plan = RLNetwork(~hyper, [\x, \y, \z].collect { |n| [n, ~cell] }, [[\x, \y, \i2], [\y, \z, \i5], [\x, \z, \t3]]);
~top = ~plan.fillings(\x, ~cell).first;                               // the network of networks that starts on the cell
~top.objects.collect(_.objects);                                      // [[1, 3, 7], [1, 11, 7], [4, 6, 10]]
~moves.collect { |m| m[1].arrowMap } == [~pcs.isography([\T11, \I4]), ~pcs.isography([\T11, \I10]), ~pcs.isography([\T1, \I6])];   // true

// one level up: the moves carried to the networks, and the plan again. 27 notes
~up = ~moves.collect { |m| [m[0], RLAutomorphism.onNetworks(~hyper, m[1])] };
~hyper2 = RLRelationContext.networks(~top, ~up);
~top3 = RLNetwork(~hyper2, [\x, \y, \z].collect { |n| [n, ~top] }, [[\x, \y, \i2], [\y, \z, \i5], [\x, \z, \t3]]).fillings(\x, ~top).first;
::

section:: Other groups on the triads

T, I, P, L, R come from one group, the dihedral one. link::Classes/RLExtension:: gives the others that move the 2n triads as well: once a base chord is chosen every triad is an element code::[root, type]::, and two numbers (p, q) say how elements multiply. In 12 steps there are eight such groups, in a prime division two. Multiplying on the left or on the right gives the two kinds of move, and the base chord can itself be moved.

code::
~space = RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(12));
RLExtension.classes(12);                              // eight (p, q)
~ext = RLExtension(12, 6, 11);
~ext.right([3, 1], ~space.minor(7), ~space.major(0));
~ext.right([3, 1], ~space.minor(7), ~space.minor(0)); // from another base chord
::

Hook's uniform transformations (link::Classes/RLWreath::) move each chord type by its own interval: P, L, R and every right action above are such; one of them repeated is a progression.

code::
~w = RLWreath(12, 2);
~w.of(RLChordContext(~space).r, ~space);              // [[9, 3], [1, 0]]: R = <9, 3, ->
~w.order([[9, 8], [1, 0]]);                           // 24: R and L in turn, through every triad
::

The eight groups need not be given: link::Classes/RLExtension#*all:: lists every extension of a division's roots by a group of types, each action with each class of cocycles. And the roots themselves may be a group that does not commute, given by its table (link::Classes/RLTableGroup::): the 48 transformations of 24 quarter-tones are T and I of 12 notes by two types, "on the grid" and "a quarter-tone off".

code::
RLExtension.all(RLEDO(12), [[0, 1], [1, 0]]).collect(_.orders);      // eight lists of orders: the eight groups
~d = RLTableGroup.dihedral(12);                                      // T and I of 12 notes
~quarter = Array.series(12) ++ Array.fill(12) { |i| 12 + ((i - 1) % 12) };
~q = RLExtension.general(~d, [[0, 1], [1, 0]], [~d.pcs, ~quarter], [[0, 0], [0, 1]]);
~q.order([0, 1]);                                                    // 24: a quarter-tone
::

section:: Chords of four tones

A chord context takes a chord type of any size with its inversion. link::Classes/RLChordSpace#*tetrads:: is the tetrad nearest 4:5:6:7 and the same upside down: a dominant and a half-diminished seventh in 12 steps, exact on the lattice of 3, 5 and 7. Each pair of tones gives one contextual inversion (K01, K02...; P, L, R are the three of a triad), and two in a row transpose a chord, so the commas of the 7-limit are short walks.

code::
~t12 = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.tetrads(RLEDO(12)));
~t12.neighbours(~t12.space.otonal(0));       // six half-diminished sevenths around C7
~t7 = RLChordContext(RLChordSpace.tetrads(RLJust([3, 5, 7])));
~word = [\K12, \K01, \K12, \K01, \K13, \K01];        // two fifths up, a harmonic seventh up
RLWalk(~t12, ~t12.space.otonal(0), ~word).isClosed;  // true
~t7.system.commaName(RLWalk(~t7, ~t7.space.otonal, ~word).drift);     // septimal comma 64/63
RLRelationContext.powerTowers.graph.size;    // 39 seventh chords, one voice by a semitone
::

In 12 steps the moves between a dominant and a half-diminished seventh that keep two tones and move two by a semitone are Childs's operations. They are steps of this context on the right: six wechsels (five contextual inversions, and the sixth after a tritone) when the two voices go the same way, three schritts (by 3, 6, 9) when they go opposite ways (link::Classes/RLChordContext#-wechsel::). Together they are the nine edges at each chord of Pipeline (link::Classes/RLRelationContext#*pipeline::).

The other seventh chords (major, minor, diminished) are their own inversions, so they have no chord context. They come as relations (Power Towers above) and as uniform transformations: link::Classes/RLWreath#-census:: finds, between five types, the 17 swaps that move one voice by one or two semitones (Cannas, Antonini and Pernazza), which generate every permutation of the types with every list of shifts that sums to zero.

code::
~t12.k(1, 2) == ~t12.wechsel(1);             // true: C7 to C# half-diminished
~t12.schritt(3).(~t12.space.otonal(0));      // Eb7: two voices by a semitone, opposite ways
RLRelationContext.pipeline.graph.degrees.asSet;     // Set[9]
RLWreath(12, 5).census(RLChordSpace.sevenths).size; // 17
::

section:: Time

Time-spans (link::Classes/RLTimeSpans::) are moved from both sides as triads are: on the left everything is stretched and shifted, on the right each span moves by its own duration. A rhythm is a walk, and the same word from another span is the same rhythm at another time and tempo. Metre is a relation (link::Classes/RLMetre::), with shifts and dilations as its automorphisms, and a canon a pair of sets that tile the cycle.

code::
~time = RLTimeSpans.new;
~rhythm = RLWalk(~time, [0, 1], ~time.word([1, 0.5, 0.5, 1, 2]));
~rhythm.chords;                                            // [0, 1], [1, 0.5], [1.5, 0.5], [2, 1], [3, 2]
RLWalk(~time, [0, 1.5], ~rhythm.word).chords;              // the same, one and a half times slower
RLMetre(12).complements([0, 1, 5]);                        // [[0, 3, 6, 9]]
::

The voices of a canon are one rhythm under several left steps: code::[u, d]:: is the rhythm shifted by u and d times as slow. A canon by augmentation can tile the pulses too, each voice at its own speed.

code::
~time.canon(~rhythm.chords, [[0, 1], [0, 2]]);             // two voices: the rhythm, and the same twice as slow
~time.endTogether(~rhythm.chords, 0.5);                    // [2.5, 0.5]: twice as fast, entering at 2.5, ends with it
RLMetre.perfect([0, 1, 2], 5);                             // 15 pulses, three notes in a row at the stretches 1, 2, 4, 5, 7
::

On the line of time a pulse (link::Classes/RLPulse::) is the relation between time points a whole number of periods apart. Pulses compose to their greatest common divisor, and one is included in another when its period is a whole multiple of the other's: a metre is a chain of pulses each included in the next. A metre network (Popoff and Yust) is a network of time points on link::Classes/RLTimePoints::, and metre is at odds with itself in the two ways one network is carried into another (link::Classes/RLNetwork#-isMorphismTo::): a pulse included in a finer one (a grouping), and a shift of time (a displacement).

code::
// Brahms, "Auf dem See", bars 7 and 8, the bar as the unit
~points = RLTimePoints.new;
~top = RLNetwork(~points, [[\E, 5/2], [\G, 3]], [[\E, \G, 1/2]]);
~voice = RLNetwork(~points, [[\E, 5/2], [\F, 17/6], [\G, 3]], [[\E, \F, 1/6], [\F, \G, 1/6]]);
~piano = RLNetwork(~points, [[\E, 31/12], [\F, 17/6], [\G, 37/12]], [[\E, \F, 1/4], [\F, \G, 1/4]]);
~top.isMorphismTo(~voice);                                 // true: the half bar in three eighths
~top.isMorphismTo(~piano, nil, ~points.shift(1/12));       // true: a sixteenth later, in two dotted eighths
~points.dissonance([1/6, 5/2], [1/4, 31/12]);              // grouping
~points.common([1/6, 5/2], [1/4, 31/12]);                  // [0.5, 2.8333333333]: the two layers meet on F
RLPulse.paths(6, 1);                                       // [6, 3, 1] and [6, 2, 1]: the two metres of a hemiola

// the article's own networks join two points by a beat and by its division at once: a free network
~both = RLNetwork(~points, [[\E, 5/2], [\F, 17/6], [\G, 3]], [[\E, \F, 1/6], [\F, \G, 1/6], [\E, \G, 1/2]]);
~both.isValid;                                             // false: read strictly, two routes must compose alike
~both.free.isValid;                                        // true
// and its morphisms carry a relation at each node: a displacement said with M1/12 there, and no map of time
~later = RLNetwork(~points, [[\E, 31/12], [\G, 37/12]], [[\E, \G, 1/2]]);
~top.isMorphismTo(~later, nil, nil, 1/12);                 // true
::

section:: Graded belonging

For a monoid of maps that keep a chord, "this note belongs to the chord" has more answers than yes and no: the maps that bring the note into it (link::Classes/RLRelationContext#*preserving::, Noll's monoid). Their share is a number between 0 and 1 for every pitch class, to use as control data.

code::
~noll = RLRelationContext.preserving(RLEDO(12), [0, 4, 7]);
~noll.belonging([0, 4, 7]);          // C, E, G: 1; Eb: 0.875; Ab, B, C#, F#, A, Bb: 0.625; D, F: 0.375
::

section:: Sound

link::Classes/RLWalk#-freqs:: gives the voicings as frequencies, link::Classes/RLWalk#-asPattern:: as a pattern of chords:

code::
Pbind(\freq, ~pump.asPattern(nil, 220), \dur, 1, \legato, 1).play;
Pbind(\freq, ~lifted.asPattern(nil, 220), \dur, 1, \legato, 1).play;    // ends 21.5 cents below its start
::

In a piece, a walk is played through a beat of reCurrent, and a walk without an end by an link::Classes/RLWalker::: it keeps the chord and the voices, and takes one step for each name a pattern gives. The word can change on the next beat; the harmony goes on from where it is.

code::
~walker = RLWalker(~ctx, ~c, [60, 64, 67], 8.1757989156, 0.75);
~beat = ~song.layer(\core).addBeat(\harmony, [instrument: \default, dur_flex: 2, freq: ~walker.asPattern(Pseq([\R, \L], inf))]);
~beat.set(\freq, ~walker.asPattern(Prand([\P, \L, \R], inf)));
// a rhythm from time-spans as the beat's durations
~beat.set(\dur_flex, ~rhythm.chords.collect(_[1]));
::

HomewareSC's code::Math/Category_Theory/studies/:: holds the studies rendered offline and measured (one walk in 12, 19, 31 steps and just intonation; walks on chord graphs; networks moved by automorphisms; tetrads; canons; networks of networks; metres).

section:: Pictures

Pages of HomewareVisuals draw what the classes compute, fed through link::Classes/RCVisuals:: (at once, or from a beat as each chord sounds):

list::
## the canon page (code::/canon/::, link::Classes/RLCanonPage::): a canon as bars, one row for each voice, and which voice starts a note on each pulse; the pulse layers of a metre as rows (code::RLCanonPage.layers::);
## the network page (code::/network/::, link::Classes/RLNetworkPage::): networks side by side, a network moved again and again by an automorphism as a row of frames; a network of networks as its networks, then its plan (code::RLNetworkPage.nested::);
## the lattice page (code::/lattice/::, link::Classes/RLLattice::): a walk of triads on the plane of fifths and major thirds, and wrapped into the torus of an equal division or the cylinder of a temperament of one comma; a walk of the tetrads 4:5:6:7 with the seventh as a third axis;
## the chord graph page (code::/chordgraph/::, link::Classes/RLChordGraph::): any link::Classes/RLGraph:: laid out here (by voice-leading zones, on a circle...), a walk lighting its edges, the voices below.
::

code::
RLLattice.show(~pump);
~cd = RLRelationContext.cubeDance;  ~g = RLGraph.voiceLeading(~cd.union);
RLChordGraph.show(~g, \zones, "Cube Dance", ~cd);
~path = ~g.hamiltonianCycle;
RLChordGraph.walk(~g, ~path, RLVoiceLeading.through(~path, [60, 64, 67]));
::

section:: Tests

code::tests/reLation_tests/:: (UnitTest, no server), run with HomewareSC's code::scripts/test.sh vikn::. They hold the tables of the theory: the vals of seven divisions, the cycles in each, the commas of closed walks on the lattice, the voice drifts, the least voice leading checked against every assignment; the monoids of relations (7, 4, 8, 6 and 40 arrows), the Hamiltonian cycles (62, 1296, 7776) and the two distances compared over every pair of chords; the networks and isographies of Popoff's posts (Webern, Gesualdo, "Take a Bow", the sequence his code prints) and the automorphisms (48 and 342 for T and I, 12 for U, P, L).
